<interrogationes unitas="vates">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I send one message to Claude and read the text of the reply?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_aperire vates_petitio_creare vates_nuntium_addere vates_textum_addere vates_mittere" via="lib/quaestio.c">Open
      a provider with vates_aperire(piscina, provisor, clavis,
      &amp;optiones) (provisor anthropic or fictus; an unknown name
      returns NIHIL and lists the known ones on stderr), build a request
      with vates_petitio_creare(piscina, exemplar), add a user turn
      with vates_nuntium_addere(p, VATES_USOR) and
      vates_textum_addere, then call vates_mittere(v, p, propositum,
      piscina). r-&gt;bloci is a Xar of VatesBlocus STRUCTS
      (xar_obtinere gives a VatesBlocus*); collect textus from blocks
      whose genus is VATES_TEXTUS. Text you add is NOT copied: the
      chorda must stay valid while the request is in use.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I run a tool-use loop with vates?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_instrumentum_addere vates_responsum_addere vates_effectum_addere vates_nuntium_addere">Declare
      tools with vates_instrumentum_addere(p, &amp;instrumentum), a
      VatesInstrumentum holding titulus, descriptio and a JSON Schema
      JsonValor (copied by value, so the chordae must outlive p). When r-&gt;causa_finis is
      VATES_FINIS_INSTRUMENTUM, append the reply with
      vates_responsum_addere(p, r) (every block, OPACUM included,
      becomes an assistant turn), then open a VATES_USOR turn and add
      one vates_effectum_addere(n, id, contentum, erratum) per
      VATES_INSTRUMENTUM_PETITUM block, using that block's id. Send
      the same petitio again.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tell a failed call from a successful one?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_mittere vates_error_descriptio VatesResponsum">vates_mittere
      never returns NIHIL (except with a NIHIL piscina): check
      r-&gt;successus. On failure r-&gt;error says which kind (RETE,
      TEMPUS, STATUS, PARSE, or LIMES for a local refusal before
      anything was sent: NIHIL vates or petitio, too many cache
      points), status_http the HTTP code, and error_genus and
      error_nuntius carry the provider's own error type (such as
      overloaded_error) and message; when the error body is not JSON,
      error_nuntius is its first CCLVI bytes. Only status CC counts as
      success. vates_error_descriptio names the
      kind. A refusal is a SUCCESS with causa_finis
      VATES_FINIS_RECUSATIO; recusatio_categoria holds
      stop_details.category when the API sends it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does vates_mittere retry, and how long can it block?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_mittere vates_optiones_ordinariae VatesOptiones">Up
      to conatus_maximi attempts (0 means III). Network errors are
      always retried, a timeout only on the first attempt (a long call
      is expensive to repeat), and HTTP 408, 409, 429 and every status
      from D up. Between attempts it sleeps for the retry-after
      seconds or mora_iterandi_ms (0 means II s), always capped by
      mora_iterandi_maxima_ms (0 means LX s); each attempt has
      tempus_ms (0 means X minutes). For the three durations zero or a
      negative value means the default, so the sleep cannot be
      switched off (use I ms).
      With the defaults one call can block about XXX minutes (III
      attempts of X minutes) plus the sleeps. r-&gt;usus is summed
      over all attempts and r-&gt;conatus counts them. The streaming
      calls retry only before the first delta, and a stream that
      returned CC is retried only for an overloaded_error or api_error
      event.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can vates read the API key from the environment for me?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_aperire vates_anthropic_aperire">No, by
      design: the caller reads the key and passes its bytes as clavis.
      Vates copies it once, NUL-terminated, for the x-api-key header,
      and never writes it to the ledger, the herbarium captures or any
      error message (a test reads the ledger and every herbarium file
      back to prove it). The fictus provider ignores the key.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I know what a call cost?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_mittere VatesPretium VatesUsus">Give
      VatesOptiones.pretia (a VatesPretium table with micro-dollars per
      million tokens for input, output, cache read and 5m and 1h cache
      writes) and pretia_numerus. Prices come from the caller because
      they change; they are never compiled in. r-&gt;usus.pretium is
      then the cost in micro-dollars, or -I when the model is not in
      the table; the model is matched exactly against the name the API
      returns (r-&gt;exemplar), falling back to the request's
      exemplar. Set rationarium_via to also append one JSON line per
      attempt (time, model, purpose, status, tokens, price, latency),
      written with fsync.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I use prompt caching?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_systema_addere vates_textum_addere vates_petitio_caudam_signare vates_mittere">Mark
      a breakpoint with the signum_thesauri argument of
      vates_systema_addere, or set signum_thesauri on a VatesBlocus
      returned by vates_textum_addere or vates_effectum_addere;
      vates_petitio_caudam_signare adds the automatic tail point. The
      API allows IV breakpoints, so more than IV makes the call fail
      with VATES_ERROR_LIMES before anything is sent. OPACUM blocks
      are never marked and are not counted. The request body is
      byte-deterministic, so an unchanged prefix hits the
      cache.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I stream the reply as it is generated?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_mittere_rivum vates_vocare_rivum vates_vocatio_responsum vates_vocationem_abrumpere">vates_mittere_rivum
      blocks like vates_mittere but calls your VatesRivus with each
      text delta (cogitatio FALSUM) or thinking delta (VERUM); the
      chorda is invalid once the callback returns. The final response
      is the same as the non-streaming one. It retries only before
      the first delta; a stream that ends without message_stop is
      VATES_ERROR_RETE, and an error event is VATES_ERROR_STATUS.
      vates_vocare_rivum does the same on your Reactor without
      blocking (retry waits are reactor timers); it returns NIHIL only
      when an argument is NIHIL and reports every failure, LIMES included, through finis; then read
      vates_vocatio_responsum. finis is meant to run exactly once
      (vates_vocationem_abrumpere calls it synchronously), but if the
      reactor has no free timer slot when a retry or the LIMES
      refusal is scheduled it never runs, and vates_vocatio_finita
      stays FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test code that calls vates without the network?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_fictus_aperire vates_fictus_textum vates_fictus_instrumentum vates_fictus_crudum vates_fictus_rivum vates_fictus_petitio vates_vocare_rivum">Open
      the fictus provider (vates_fictus_aperire, or vates_aperire with
      provisor fictus), queue replies with vates_fictus_textum,
      vates_fictus_instrumentum, vates_fictus_crudum (any status and
      body) or vates_fictus_rivum, then read the exact bodies sent
      with vates_fictus_petitio. Replies are rendered as real
      Anthropic JSON and parsed by the real reader. An exhausted queue
      is a network error (fictus exhaustum), which IS retried: each
      retry sleeps mora_iterandi_ms (II s when 0) and records one more
      request, so set conatus_maximi to I or mora_iterandi_ms to I.
      Fictus only intercepts vates_mittere and vates_mittere_rivum:
      vates_vocare_rivum calls http_vocare directly, so on a fictus
      Vates it goes to the real URL.
      Set sine_herbario too, or any reply the judge finds unusual
      (non-CC status, unknown field or block) is captured to disk
      under host fictus.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the herbarium, and why do files appear in ~/.rhubarb/herbarium?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_anthropic_aperire herbarium_sedes_ordinaria" via="lib/vates.worklog.md">Response
      capture, ON by default: a judge inside the transport saves any
      response that is not an ordinary 200 (other status, non-JSON
      body, unknown stop_reason, a field or block the declared schema
      in lib/vates_responsum.norma does not know) as a specimen before
      vates reads it; a CC text/event-stream body is not judged
      yet. The
      directory is herbarium_via used as given, else
      $RHUBARB_HERBARIUM or ~/.rhubarb/herbarium plus /host. The host
      is always that of the built-in API URL (api.anthropic.com), even
      when VatesOptiones.url points elsewhere; fictus uses host
      fictus. sine_herbario VERUM turns it off. vates_vocare_rivum
      bypasses the transport and is never captured.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is an OPACUM block, and why does vates keep blocks it does not understand?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_responsum_addere vates_anthropic_corpus">Nothing
      is thrown away. Every response block keeps its raw JSON in
      crudum with provisor anthropic; every type other than text and
      tool_use (thinking with its signature, and any type vates does
      not know) becomes VATES_OPACUM. When the block goes back in
      the next request (vates_responsum_addere) an Anthropic block is
      emitted VERBATIM, so signatures and unmodelled fields survive.
      The Anthropic request builder omits an OPACUM block whose
      provisor is not anthropic, as the API itself omits another
      model's thinking.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use an API feature or beta header that vates does not model?
    <responsum symbolum="vates_extra_ponere vates_caput_addere">Yes:
      vates_extra_ponere(p, provisor, clavis, valor) adds a body field
      (emitted last, after messages) and vates_caput_addere(p,
      provisor, titulus, valor) adds a header such as anthropic-beta.
      Both are tagged with a provider name and applied only by that
      provider's request builder, so code that sets Anthropic extras
      runs unchanged, without them, under another provider. The
      fictus provider uses the Anthropic builder, so it does apply
      extras and headers tagged anthropic.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
